From ea9c2647cddc90d6e0c31bfc7ea4372c00c40445 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: =?UTF-8?q?Ville=20Kivel=C3=A4?= <ville.d.kivela@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 21 Sep 2026 22:08:26 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] Serve web accessible resources to ordinary pages

A page cannot load a resource an extension declares in
web_accessible_resources, by fetch, as an image or as a script. The
request never reaches the browser as itself: it arrives as
chrome-extension://invalid/.

There are two copies of the renderer's resource policy.
ExtensionsRendererClient owns a ResourceRequestPolicy, is told which
extensions have loaded through OnExtensionLoaded, and answers
WillSendRequest from it. ExtensionsRendererClientQt creates a second
ResourceRequestPolicyQt and overrides WillSendRequest to use that one.
Nothing calls OnExtensionLoaded on it, so its set of ids with web
accessible resources is always empty, CanRequestResource refuses every
request, and the renderer rewrites the URL to
kExtensionInvalidRequestURL. The Qt copy predates the base class owning
one.

Remove the copy and the override so the base class answers. Its
WillSendRequest also takes upstream_url, which
ContentRendererClientQt::WillSendRequest already receives and drops, so
a resource reached through a redirect is now allowed by the extension
that redirected to it.

This breaks a class of extension rather than an API: one whose scripts
are all declared in the manifest never notices, while one that declares
a small loader and imports its real bundle gets nothing into the page.

Fixes: QTBUG-150590
Pick-to: 6.140
Change-Id: I290d735d1de6abef30b967417f9e5861d9a9c0e1
Reviewed-by: Allan Sandfeld Jensen <allan.jensen@qt.io>

